INT. DRIVE-IN PROJECTION BOOTH - NIGHT

A cramped cinderblock room lit by the glow of the projector. The reel clatters. Below, through a small window, a sea of parked cars and a flickering screen.

TEO MARCHETTI, 16, skinny, in a paper hat from the snack bar, threads film with careful hands. Behind him, LULA BRIGHTWATER, 16, braids and a denim jacket, sits on an upturned crate, swinging her feet.

LULA
You said your uncle wouldn't be here tonight.

TEO
He isn't. He's in Tulsa.

LULA
Then why are you sweating?

TEO
It's a hundred degrees in here.

HAMID ZAIDI, 17, Teo's best friend, slouches in the doorway with a bag of popcorn and a smirk. He has not been invited.

HAMID
Marchetti. Heard you were showing a double feature up here. Me and Lula.

TEO
Nobody said that.

HAMID
Lula said it. At the pool.

Lula stops swinging her feet. Teo looks at her. She looks at the floor.

LULA
I said we might hang out. That's different.

HAMID
It's the same thing, apparently.

TEO
Hamid, go back downstairs.

HAMID
You can't tell me what to do. You're a guy in a paper hat.

Teo's jaw tightens. He turns off the second projector and stands, gripping the reel in his fist like something he might throw.

TEO
Say it again.

LULA
Teo, don't.

MRS. ESTELLE VANTERPOOL, 60s, the theater owner, in a cardigan and orthopedic shoes, appears in the doorway behind Hamid. She taps a flashlight against her palm.

MRS. VANTERPOOL
Mr. Marchetti. Why is the screen showing a blank sheet?

Teo drops the reel. It rolls across the floor and stops at her feet.

TEO
It's my fault, Mrs. Vanterpool. All of it.

She picks it up, studies the three of them, and hands the reel back to Teo.

MRS. VANTERPOOL
Then you can fix it. And your friends can buy their own popcorn.
